In mastering complex skills like business English, absolutely.

731
00:38:42,360 --> 00:38:42,450
Yeah.

732
00:38:42,470 --> 00:38:47,740
And in achieving that kind of true professional resilience, you know, being able to handle the pressure.

733
00:38:47,940 --> 00:38:56,920
So that strong inner voice we talked about, the trained one, it translates directly into real, tangible, compounding results over time.

734
00:38:57,100 --> 00:38:59,500
That's the key takeaway, compound results.

735
00:38:59,520 --> 00:39:11,780
Okay, so now that we've hopefully nailed down what discipline really is, the skill of cognitive control, this trainable muscle, let's turn to its, well, its unreliable cousin, motivation.

736
00:39:11,820 --> 00:39:12,900
Ah, motivation.

737
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:14,140
Everyone loves motivation.

738
00:39:14,260 --> 00:39:21,190
Everyone loves it, but why is discipline actually more essential, more critical than motivation for sustained long-term success?

739
00:39:21,330 --> 00:39:22,750
This is a really important point, I think.

740
00:39:22,790 --> 00:39:23,090
Yeah.

741
00:39:23,110 --> 00:39:25,010
It's where people often get stuck, right?

742
00:39:25,030 --> 00:39:28,300
They wait around, hoping for motivation to strike them like lightning.

743
00:39:28,500 --> 00:39:30,920
Like waiting for inspiration to hit a painter, yeah.

744
00:39:31,780 --> 00:39:36,610
But as we sort of touched on, motivation is fundamentally inconsistent.

745
00:39:37,190 --> 00:39:38,390
It's basically a mood.

746
00:39:38,820 --> 00:39:40,200
It feels good when it's there, though.

747
00:39:40,460 --> 00:39:40,840
It does.

748
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:42,540
But it's more than just a mood, actually.

749
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:44,470
It's chemically quite volatile.

750
00:39:44,770 --> 00:39:45,190
How so?

751
00:39:45,730 --> 00:39:49,500
Well, motivation is often tied to novelty, something new.

752
00:39:49,480 --> 00:39:51,600
and a surge of dopamine in the brain.

753
00:39:51,860 --> 00:39:53,090
Okay, the reward chemical.

754
00:39:53,250 --> 00:39:53,750
Exactly.

755
00:39:53,850 --> 00:40:04,130
So when you start a new project, let's say a new business English course, your brain registers the novelty, it sees the potential reward, and boom, it releases dopamine.

756
00:40:04,190 --> 00:40:06,250
That gives you that spark of excitement.

757
00:40:06,330 --> 00:40:07,420
I feel energized.

758
00:40:07,480 --> 00:40:08,140
I want to do it.

759
00:40:08,240 --> 00:40:11,080
But the brain adapts incredibly quickly.

760
00:40:11,060 --> 00:40:15,410
When the novelty wears off, which is usually within a few days or maybe a couple of weeks.

761
00:40:15,550 --> 00:40:17,970
That chemical flood just recedes.

762
00:40:18,090 --> 00:40:19,030
It recedes, yeah.

763
00:40:19,540 --> 00:40:21,860
And the motivation often crashes right along with it.

764
00:40:22,420 --> 00:40:33,580
So if our progress relies only on that inconsistent, chemically driven spark, well, studying or doing the hard work just won't happen very often, will it?

765
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:34,900
Probably not consistently, no.

766
00:40:35,080 --> 00:40:38,400
We're essentially setting ourselves up for failure if that's all we rely on.

767
00:40:38,470 --> 00:40:39,330
That's a bit depressing.

768
00:40:39,490 --> 00:40:40,570
Well, it doesn't have to be.

769
00:40:40,610 --> 00:40:43,740
We can use those compelling analogies from our sources here.

770
00:40:43,780 --> 00:40:45,020
Think of it this way.

771
00:40:45,500 --> 00:40:47,480
Motivation is like a spark.

772
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:49,140
It's brilliant.

773
00:40:49,420 --> 00:40:51,090
It's exciting when it happens.

774
00:40:51,510 --> 00:40:53,750
It's a great way to get the initial fire started.

775
00:40:54,230 --> 00:40:58,360
But discipline, discipline is the slow burning fire itself.

776
00:40:58,380 --> 00:41:04,860
It's the consistent heat generated every single day that keeps the whole system running, keeps you warm.

777
00:41:04,930 --> 00:41:07,730
You can't really warm a house with just a single spark, can you?

778
00:41:07,910 --> 00:41:08,540
Exactly.

779
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:10,380
It feels good for a second, but then it's gone.

780
00:41:10,500 --> 00:41:10,820
Okay.

781
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:13,730
I hear you arguing that motivation is unreliable.

782
00:41:14,930 --> 00:41:18,960
But surely motivation must matter at the start, doesn't it?

783
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:24,070
How do we use that initial excitement without becoming totally dependent on it?

784
00:41:24,270 --> 00:41:25,570
That's a really critical question.

785
00:41:25,650 --> 00:41:30,460
And yes, motivation is absolutely crucial for initiation, for getting started.

786
00:41:30,520 --> 00:41:31,440
Getting you to the starting line.

787
00:41:31,480 --> 00:41:31,980
Precisely.

788
00:41:32,020 --> 00:41:33,020
It gets you to the starting line.

789
00:41:33,500 --> 00:41:41,280
But, and this is the key part, as soon as you are moving, discipline must immediately take over the steering wheel for sustainment to keep you going.

790
00:41:41,620 --> 00:41:41,720
Okay.

791
00:41:41,740 --> 00:41:42,700
Discipline takes over.

792
00:41:42,830 --> 00:41:46,900
Let's maybe deepen the understanding with that Ali example from the sources.

793
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:49,900
We can put it into a professional learning context.

794
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:50,220
All right.

795
00:41:50,830 --> 00:41:57,900
Let's imagine Ali is really motivated initially to master complex financial English vocabulary.

796
00:41:58,640 --> 00:42:00,050
He needs it for his job.

797
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:00,520
Okay.

798
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:04,360
So on Monday, Ollie feels super excited.

799
00:42:04,440 --> 00:42:10,610
He dives in, spends maybe two solid hours studying, translating technical financial reports.

800
00:42:10,770 --> 00:42:11,230
Feels great.

801
00:42:11,630 --> 00:42:12,910
Massive wave of accomplishment.

802
00:42:12,930 --> 00:42:13,630
Huge effort.

803
00:42:13,690 --> 00:42:15,530
This is motivation at its absolute peak.

804
00:42:16,090 --> 00:42:17,570
But then Tuesday comes around.

805
00:42:17,630 --> 00:42:19,590
He has a difficult day at work.

806
00:42:19,610 --> 00:42:20,320
He's tired.

807
00:42:20,360 --> 00:42:23,020
The material doesn't feel quite so new and exciting anymore.

808
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:25,080
So he skips it just for today.

809
00:42:25,260 --> 00:42:25,540
Okay.

810
00:42:25,620 --> 00:42:26,740
Happens to the best of us.

811
00:42:26,770 --> 00:42:29,700
But then by Wednesday, he feels a bit guilty about missing Tuesday.

812
00:42:29,740 --> 00:42:34,640
The gap feels wider now, harder to bridge, and he just puts it off entirely, forgets about it.

813
00:42:34,840 --> 00:42:35,330
And the result?

814
00:42:35,710 --> 00:42:39,690
Zero sustained learning over the course of the week, just that one big burst on Monday.

815
00:42:39,930 --> 00:42:40,130
Right.

816
00:42:40,170 --> 00:42:41,920
Now contrast that with the disciplined learner.

817
00:42:42,460 --> 00:42:43,320
How do they approach it?

818
00:42:43,760 --> 00:42:46,260
The disciplined learner, well, they don't wait for the feeling.

819
00:42:46,550 --> 00:42:48,410
They recognize the psychological trap here.

820
00:42:48,650 --> 00:42:50,530
There's something called time discounting.

821
00:42:50,690 --> 00:42:51,470
Time discounting.

822
00:42:51,510 --> 00:42:52,050
What's that?

823
00:42:52,150 --> 00:43:07,250
It's our tendency as humans to value immediate smaller rewards like rest or scrolling social media or watching TV much more highly than future larger rewards like achieving fluency or getting that promotion.

824
00:43:07,690 --> 00:43:10,430
So we prefer the small win now over the big win later.

825
00:43:10,630 --> 00:43:11,870
We do instinctively.

826
00:43:12,150 --> 00:43:14,080
And the discipline learner knows this.

827
00:43:14,140 --> 00:43:18,540
They know that consistency is the only way to really combat that time discounting effect.

828
00:43:18,780 --> 00:43:19,660
How does their week look?

829
00:43:20,000 --> 00:43:21,720
They study a little bit every single day.

830
00:43:21,780 --> 00:43:25,680
Maybe it's just 20 minutes, but it's 20 minutes of intense, focused practice.

831
00:43:26,060 --> 00:43:30,530
And they do it regardless of whether they feel excited or tired or bored or stressed.

832
00:43:30,750 --> 00:43:33,010
That steady, smaller investment adds up.

833
00:43:33,030 --> 00:43:33,530
Exactly.

834
00:43:33,630 --> 00:43:39,140
It's far, far better in the long run than relying on those rare, intense spikes of motivation Ali had.

835
00:43:39,620 --> 00:43:45,570
So real progress then, the kind that actually translates into career advancement or genuine fluency in English.

836
00:43:45,590 --> 00:43:45,810
Yeah.

837
00:43:46,210 --> 00:43:49,530
It doesn't come from those single explosive days of excitement.

838
00:43:49,590 --> 00:43:49,950
No.

839
00:43:49,980 --> 00:43:56,180
It comes from being relentlessly, almost boringly, predictably consistent.

840
00:43:56,220 --> 00:43:57,360
Boringly consistent.

841
00:43:57,430 --> 00:43:57,930
I like that.

842
00:43:58,050 --> 00:44:02,750
And here's what the sources suggest is the real secret of success when you look at high achievers.

843
00:44:03,350 --> 00:44:12,090
Successful people, CEOs, top athletes, specialized writers, leaders in any field, they are not necessarily more motivated than everyone else.

844
00:44:12,490 --> 00:44:15,450
They might not feel like working out or practicing any more than we do.

845
00:44:15,650 --> 00:44:15,910
Really?

846
00:44:16,070 --> 00:44:16,850
So what's the difference?

847
00:44:16,950 --> 00:44:18,910
They are simply more disciplined.

848
00:44:19,490 --> 00:44:23,430
They have built systems, routines, habits to ensure they show up.

849
00:44:23,810 --> 00:44:28,600
They stick to the routine and they do the work regardless of their momentary emotional state.

850
00:44:28,760 --> 00:44:31,720
They treat their commitments like non-negotiable appointments.

851
00:44:31,740 --> 00:44:32,750
Exactly like that.

852
00:44:32,830 --> 00:44:34,770
It's something they do like brushing their teeth.

853
00:44:34,870 --> 00:44:37,840
This is really the critical call to you, the listener, isn't it?

854
00:44:37,990 --> 00:44:38,380
Yeah.

855
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:45,530
Your goal shouldn't be to feel constantly excited about learning complex grammar rules or tackling tedious market analysis in English.

856
00:44:45,690 --> 00:44:46,710
No, definitely not.

857
00:44:46,770 --> 00:44:55,060
If you wait for the perfect mood to strike, you're basically surrendering control of your progress to your transient feelings, which change all the time.

858
00:44:55,450 --> 00:44:58,010
So the real goal is to build a steady system.

859
00:44:58,140 --> 00:44:58,940
Build the system.

860
00:44:59,570 --> 00:45:01,230
Practice even when it feels hard.

861
00:45:01,570 --> 00:45:08,110
And let the discipline, the routine, carry you forward when that motivation inevitably, predictably fades.

862
00:45:08,450 --> 00:45:09,150
It will fade.

863
00:45:09,570 --> 00:45:10,180
Count on it.

864
00:45:10,510 --> 00:45:18,120
OK, so if motivation is this unreliable spark, where does our sustained energy actually come from?

865
00:45:18,200 --> 00:45:19,100
How do we keep going?

866
00:45:19,580 --> 00:45:22,100
The sources seem to point really clearly towards automation.

867
00:45:22,220 --> 00:45:22,680
That's right.

868
00:45:22,780 --> 00:45:26,970
We need to take the internal debate, the choice out of the equation as much as possible.

869
00:45:27,450 --> 00:45:30,270
And that brings us right to the power of daily habits.

870
00:45:30,350 --> 00:45:30,930
Habits.

871
00:45:30,950 --> 00:45:34,640
Habits are really the practical boots on the ground application of discipline.

872
00:45:34,940 --> 00:45:36,940
They are small, automatic actions.

873
00:45:36,920 --> 00:45:42,430
Things you perform without having to consciously engage that high-level executive functioning we talked about.

874
00:45:42,750 --> 00:45:44,290
So you don't have to think hard about them.

875
00:45:44,310 --> 00:45:44,890
Exactly.

876
00:45:45,250 --> 00:45:51,060
When a task becomes a habit, we actually move its execution to a different part of the brain, the basal ganglia.

877
00:45:51,140 --> 00:45:51,900
Basal ganglia.

878
00:45:52,060 --> 00:45:52,300
Okay.

879
00:45:52,480 --> 00:45:54,830
That's the part that handles routines and automation.

880
00:45:55,650 --> 00:46:04,400
And this is brilliant because it saves your limited supply of willpower, your executive function energy, for the truly challenging decisions you face during the day.

881
00:46:04,520 --> 00:46:05,860
That is fascinating.

882
00:46:06,760 --> 00:46:13,530
So the goal is basically to make the necessary actions so automatic that you don't even have to fight yourself to start them.

883
00:46:13,750 --> 00:46:14,750
That's the ideal, yeah.

884
00:46:15,030 --> 00:46:19,860
So how do we start building these simple but hopefully high-impact automatic habits?

885
00:46:20,420 --> 00:46:20,980
Where do we begin?

886
00:46:21,340 --> 00:46:24,220
We start small, maybe even deceptively small.

887
00:46:24,700 --> 00:46:29,790
Take the example mentioned in the sources of making your bed every single morning.

888
00:46:29,770 --> 00:46:30,530
Making your bed.

889
00:46:30,870 --> 00:46:31,170
Really?

890
00:46:31,490 --> 00:46:34,200
A lot of people would dismiss that as totally trivial, wouldn't they?

891
00:46:34,640 --> 00:46:39,030
Does that really build discipline for, say, learning financial English?

892
00:46:39,180 --> 00:46:40,310
It sounds trivial, I know.

893
00:46:40,630 --> 00:46:44,470
But the sources emphasize the immediate psychological win it provides.

894
00:46:44,550 --> 00:46:45,590
A psychological win.

895
00:46:45,630 --> 00:46:46,140
Yeah.

896
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:49,440
It creates an organized feeling in just one small part of your life.

897
00:46:50,180 --> 00:46:54,890
And it signals to your brain that the day has started with a successful, completed task.

898
00:46:55,330 --> 00:46:56,050
It sets a tone.

899
00:46:56,390 --> 00:46:58,090
It's like a tiny accomplishment first thing.

900
00:46:58,240 --> 00:46:58,820
Exactly.

901
00:46:58,900 --> 00:47:00,610
It's something called psychological priming.

902
00:47:01,090 --> 00:47:08,230
It sets the tone and actually makes you slightly more likely to take on the next discipline task, whatever that might be.

903
00:47:08,490 --> 00:47:09,510
OK, I can see that.

904
00:47:10,050 --> 00:47:14,240
But I do worry slightly that focusing on things that small.

905
00:47:14,220 --> 00:47:24,130
might detract from tackling the real difficult work, you know, preparing for that global team meeting or writing a detailed quarterly report in English.

906
00:47:24,770 --> 00:47:28,880
Isn't that just a fancy form of, well, disciplined procrastination?

907
00:47:28,980 --> 00:47:29,220
Yeah.

908
00:47:29,480 --> 00:47:30,400
Avoiding the big stuff.

909
00:47:30,640 --> 00:47:33,930
That's a really fair challenge, but the psychological principle still holds.

910
00:47:34,390 --> 00:47:35,490
You're building momentum.

911
00:47:35,910 --> 00:47:41,280
Think of making the bed as maybe the very first, very light repetition in your discipline gym session for the day.

912
00:47:41,320 --> 00:47:42,400
Okay, the warm-up rep.

913
00:47:42,420 --> 00:47:42,820
Exactly.

914
00:47:43,160 --> 00:47:45,390
It's light, but it trains the muscle of routine.

915
00:47:45,830 --> 00:47:53,080
And once you have that little bit of momentum, then you transition to the more high-impact habits, like, for instance, writing a short, focused to-do list for the day.

916
00:47:53,240 --> 00:47:57,970
And crucially, the sources recommend limiting that list, not making it endless.

917
00:47:58,130 --> 00:47:59,230
Yes, absolutely.

918
00:47:59,310 --> 00:48:02,670
Limit it to just maybe three or four highly critical items.

919
00:48:02,790 --> 00:48:03,740
Why only three or four?

920
00:48:03,720 --> 00:48:06,700
because this prevents you from feeling overwhelmed right at the start.

921
00:48:07,300 --> 00:48:14,870
And it ensures your limited energy, your focus is directed towards the professional priorities that will actually move the needle on your calls.

922
00:48:15,190 --> 00:48:23,590
Things like finalizing that presentation or dedicating specific time to learn those industry specific English negotiation phrases you need.

923
00:48:23,680 --> 00:48:24,200
Exactly.

924
00:48:24,240 --> 00:48:30,720
It gives you a sense of control over the day, which minimizes that feeling of just reacting to whatever comes your way.

925
00:48:30,920 --> 00:48:31,980
OK.

926
00:48:31,960 --> 00:48:36,160
What other kinds of high impact routines are emphasized in the sources?

927
00:48:36,180 --> 00:48:39,970
Establishing fixed routines that sort of govern your schedule is key.

928
00:48:40,280 --> 00:48:42,340
Waking up at the same time every day, for example.

929
00:48:42,380 --> 00:48:43,630
Even on weekends.

930
00:48:44,080 --> 00:48:45,900
Ideally, yes, even on weekends.

931
00:48:46,500 --> 00:48:51,130
It stabilizes your circadian rhythm, your body clock, and it anchors your day.

932
00:48:51,850 --> 00:48:53,890
Minimizing distractions is also vital.

933
00:48:54,110 --> 00:48:56,740
You have to fiercely protect your focused work time.

934
00:48:57,010 --> 00:48:58,260
Easier said than done sometimes.

935
00:48:58,460 --> 00:48:59,680
It is, but it's critical.

936
00:49:00,040 --> 00:49:03,540
That means turning off non-essential notifications on your phone and computer.

937
00:49:03,940 --> 00:49:08,250
Maybe setting specific deep work blocks on your calendar so people know not to interrupt you.

938
00:49:08,410 --> 00:49:16,210
And that suggestion of physically putting your phone in another room, that feels extreme but probably effective.

939
00:49:16,230 --> 00:49:24,360
It's very effective, especially when you're tackling a high concentration task like reviewing dense English contracts or trying to write something complex.

940
00:49:24,380 --> 00:49:26,640
Out of sight, out of mind really works.

941
00:49:26,620 --> 00:49:34,110
Okay, so that focus on fixed time and controlling your environment, that leads us nicely into this powerful concept called habit stacking, doesn't it?

942
00:49:34,230 --> 00:49:34,450
Yes.

943
00:49:35,050 --> 00:49:41,520
Habit stacking directly leverages the brains, specifically the basal ganglia's love for established routines.

944
00:49:41,660 --> 00:49:43,140
So how does it work exactly?

945
00:49:43,160 --> 00:49:46,780
Habit stacking is basically a strategy rooted in cue-based memory.

946
00:49:47,580 --> 00:49:53,830
You link a new habit, the one you want to build, to an existing habit that you already do automatically without thinking.

947
00:49:53,840 --> 00:49:56,330
Ah, so you piggyback the new habit onto an old one.

948
00:49:56,350 --> 00:49:56,830
Exactly.

949
00:49:57,050 --> 00:50:02,500
It makes it far easier to remember and eventually automate the new behavior because the cue is already built in.

950
00:50:02,760 --> 00:50:05,360
And the structure for setting it up is really simple, isn't it?

951
00:50:05,500 --> 00:50:06,320
It's like a formula.

952
00:50:06,700 --> 00:50:07,460
Beautifully simple.

953
00:50:07,500 --> 00:50:11,410
It's just after I perform my current habit, I will perform my new habit.

954
00:50:11,810 --> 00:50:16,560
Okay, give us a few concrete examples, maybe specifically related to business English learning.

955
00:50:16,670 --> 00:50:16,990
Sure.

956
00:50:17,290 --> 00:50:21,740
Let's say your existing automatic habit is pouring your morning coffee or tea.

957
00:50:21,980 --> 00:50:22,200
Okay.

958
00:50:22,220 --> 00:50:22,820
Do that every day.

959
00:50:22,980 --> 00:50:23,180
Right.

960
00:50:23,260 --> 00:50:24,840
So you stack the new habit onto it.

961
00:50:25,460 --> 00:50:32,370
After I pour my coffee, I will review five key English terms related to my industry's current quarterly earnings report.

962
00:50:32,390 --> 00:50:33,340
Just five terms.

963
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:33,720
Okay.

964
00:50:33,760 --> 00:50:34,180
Manageable.

965
00:50:34,660 --> 00:50:35,420
Another one.

966
00:50:35,440 --> 00:50:38,020
Or maybe you always commute by public transport.

967
00:50:38,040 --> 00:50:39,240
That's an existing habit.

968
00:50:39,360 --> 00:50:39,860
Exactly.

969
00:50:40,280 --> 00:50:47,270
After I sit down on the train, I will listen to an English language business news podcast for 10 minutes before you check emails or anything else.

970
00:50:47,290 --> 00:50:47,510
Okay.

971
00:50:47,570 --> 00:50:48,290
I see how that works.

972
00:50:48,690 --> 00:50:58,080
And maybe for a working professional who deals with a lot of email, maybe email fatigue, could you stack something onto a task you already do, even if it's annoying?

973
00:50:58,480 --> 00:50:59,000
Absolutely.

974
00:50:59,040 --> 00:51:00,220
That can be really effective.

975
00:51:00,660 --> 00:51:09,430
For example, after I save the final draft of a major email I've written, I will spend just five minutes reading that email back, specifically looking for and correcting

976
00:51:09,660 --> 00:51:12,550
Two common grammatical errors I know I often make.

977
00:51:13,200 --> 00:51:18,230
Ah, so you anchor the learning behavior directly onto the existing professional workflow.

978
00:51:18,250 --> 00:51:18,710
Very clever.

979
00:51:18,910 --> 00:51:21,960
It makes it part of the job, not an extra thing you have to remember.

980
00:51:22,340 --> 00:51:28,840
Now, I think maybe the most important point in this whole section, the one we really need to stress, is the starting rule.

981
00:51:28,950 --> 00:51:29,450
Yes.

982
00:51:29,470 --> 00:51:36,720
We really can't emphasize enough the importance of starting small, maybe even infinitesimally small, and building slowly.

983
00:51:36,760 --> 00:51:45,870
If you try to change everything all at once, starting Monday, I'm going to wake up at 5 a.m., exercise for an hour, study English for an hour, eat perfectly.

984
00:51:46,090 --> 00:51:46,820
Yeah, that never works.

985
00:51:46,970 --> 00:51:47,520
It never works.

986
00:51:47,580 --> 00:51:51,820
You'll quickly deplete your limited executive function capacity, your willpower.

987
00:51:52,020 --> 00:51:52,280
Yeah.

988
00:51:52,300 --> 00:51:56,230
It leads straight to burnout and usually complete collapse of the plan within days.

989
00:51:56,450 --> 00:51:58,770
So the sources stress the compound effect instead.

990
00:51:58,750 --> 00:51:59,590
Exactly.

991
00:51:59,610 --> 00:52:02,780
The power of tiny actions compounding over time.

992
00:52:03,320 --> 00:52:10,740
Even just 10 minutes of focused English practice or reading just one single paragraph of a challenging industry report in English.

993
00:52:11,600 --> 00:52:13,790
It builds that discipline muscle little by little.

994
00:52:13,820 --> 00:52:19,030
The key is making the action so small, it feels almost impossible not to do it.

995
00:52:19,140 --> 00:52:21,220
Like it's harder to skip it than to just do it.

996
00:52:21,240 --> 00:52:21,700
Precisely.

997
00:52:22,220 --> 00:52:26,430
You want to lower the bar for starting so much that you just step over it without thinking.

998
00:52:26,620 --> 00:52:28,840
So the advice is...

999
00:52:28,820 --> 00:52:57,880
choose one tiny habit this week make it absolutely non-negotiable and practice it every single day that is the true leverage point for building lasting discipline okay so habits provide the engine right the power for daily discipline but that engine needs a clear navigation system it needs to know where it's going otherwise it's just spinning its wheels exactly so now let's talk about the structure that guides that daily discipline clear goals and detailed planning

1000
00:52:57,860 --> 00:53:00,570
Discipline with that direction is just wasted effort.

1001
00:53:00,830 --> 00:53:01,010
Right.

1002
00:53:01,170 --> 00:53:04,790
And we need to understand the two distinct but complementary roles here.

1003
00:53:05,360 --> 00:53:06,760
Goals give us the direction.

1004
00:53:06,860 --> 00:53:08,700
They show us precisely where we want to go.

1005
00:53:08,840 --> 00:53:13,290
And that allows our executive functioning, our brain CEO, to prioritize effectively.

1006
00:53:13,670 --> 00:53:18,880
Because without a clear, maybe even written goal, the learner is essentially just walking without a map, aren't they?

1007
00:53:18,970 --> 00:53:19,700
Completely.

1008
00:53:19,740 --> 00:53:25,400
And that makes it incredibly easy to justify giving up when things inevitably get difficult or boring.

1009
00:53:25,620 --> 00:53:26,920
OK, so goals give the where.

1010
00:53:26,960 --> 00:53:28,110
What about planning?

1011
00:53:28,510 --> 00:53:31,590
Planning shows us the actionable, sequential steps.

1012
00:53:31,730 --> 00:53:32,670
It's the how to get there.

1013
00:53:33,270 --> 00:53:39,520
And when goals are vague or fuzzy, discipline really suffers because you don't know what specific action to take next.

1014
00:53:39,800 --> 00:53:44,920
So the sources strongly advocate for using a structured framework for setting goals, don't they?

1015
00:53:45,080 --> 00:53:46,690
Like the SMART criteria.

1016
00:53:46,700 --> 00:53:47,060
Yes.

1017
00:53:47,300 --> 00:53:48,800
Smart is a classic for a reason.

1018
00:53:48,920 --> 00:53:57,360
Your professional objectives, whether work-related or for English learning, they need to be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.

1019
00:53:57,840 --> 00:53:58,020
Okay.

1020
00:53:58,040 --> 00:53:58,980
Let's make that concrete.

1021
00:53:59,400 --> 00:54:04,570
Give us an example of a vague goal versus a smart goal, maybe in a business or English learning context.

1022
00:54:04,870 --> 00:54:05,170
Okay.

1023
00:54:05,190 --> 00:54:08,700
A vague goal is something like, I want to speak English more fluently.

1024
00:54:08,920 --> 00:54:09,860
We hear that all the time.

1025
00:54:09,940 --> 00:54:10,140
Right.

1026
00:54:10,260 --> 00:54:11,140
What does that even mean?

1027
00:54:11,480 --> 00:54:12,300
How do you measure it?

1028
00:54:12,380 --> 00:54:13,000
Exactly.

1029
00:54:13,060 --> 00:54:14,250
It's useless for discipline.

1030
00:54:14,750 --> 00:54:25,400
A discipline smart goal would be something like, I will be able to confidently lead a 30-minute discussion in English on our Q4 sales strategy without relying on notes by December 31st of this year.

1031
00:54:25,580 --> 00:54:26,080
Ah, okay.

1032
00:54:26,660 --> 00:54:38,640
That's specific, leading a discussion on Q4 sales, measurable 30 minutes without notes, achievable, presumably with effort, relevant to their job, and time-bound by December 31st.

1033
00:54:38,620 --> 00:54:39,220
Precisely.

1034
00:54:39,610 --> 00:54:44,270
That defined target gives your discipline something concrete and unambiguous to aim for each day.

1035
00:54:44,290 --> 00:54:45,960
OK, so you've set your SMART goal.

1036
00:54:46,400 --> 00:54:47,140
What's the next step?

1037
00:54:47,520 --> 00:54:48,480
The planning process.

1038
00:54:48,580 --> 00:54:48,860
Yes.

1039
00:54:49,200 --> 00:54:54,490
And the planning process must relentlessly break that big SMART goal down into much smaller pieces.

1040
00:54:55,030 --> 00:54:57,590
If the goal is, say, better business speaking skills,

1041
00:54:57,570 --> 00:54:59,740
The plan can't just be study more English.

1042
00:54:59,940 --> 00:55:01,520
No, that's still too vague.

1043
00:55:01,820 --> 00:55:10,680
It needs to be broken down into specific micro-commitments, things you can actually schedule and do, maybe things you can stack onto existing daily habits like we just discussed.

1044
00:55:10,700 --> 00:55:14,350
Okay, so what might those micro-commitments look like for the speaking goal?

1045
00:55:14,570 --> 00:55:17,930
The plan must include actionable, measurable steps.

1046
00:55:18,250 --> 00:55:25,200
For example, I will practice role playing common meeting scenarios in English for 15 minutes every weekday morning.

1047
00:55:25,640 --> 00:55:25,980
OK.

1048
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:31,070
I will watch one 10 minute English video related to my industry negotiation tactics every evening.

1049
00:55:31,310 --> 00:55:31,850
Right.

1050
00:55:31,870 --> 00:55:37,010
I will learn and practice using five new industry specific phrases related to presentations each morning.

1051
00:55:37,670 --> 00:55:41,470
This detailed plan becomes the tactical blueprint for your daily discipline.

1052
00:55:41,660 --> 00:55:45,120
And this leads us to another step the sources emphasize as being really crucial.

1053
00:55:45,980 --> 00:55:49,050
The simple physical act of writing it all down.

1054
00:55:49,160 --> 00:55:49,430
Yes.

1055
00:55:49,870 --> 00:55:55,070
Writing your goals and especially your detailed plan down on paper or in a document.

1056
00:55:55,730 --> 00:55:57,110
It does something psychologically.

1057
00:55:57,240 --> 00:56:01,180
It moves them out of the realm of just abstract wishes or daydreams.

1058
00:56:01,160 --> 00:56:02,580
And into committed reality.

1059
00:56:02,680 --> 00:56:03,280
Exactly.

1060
00:56:03,300 --> 00:56:10,570
It makes the dream feel tangible, feel real, and it transforms it into a concrete roadmap that you can physically follow and, importantly, track.

1061
00:56:10,590 --> 00:56:15,120
It's essential, then, to break that big goal down into those small, trackable steps.

1062
00:56:15,760 --> 00:56:17,350
The simpler, the better, it seems.

1063
00:56:17,580 --> 00:56:27,470
The smaller, the better, especially at the beginning, because writing it down also instantly sets the stage for what might be the most powerful element in this whole section.

1064
00:56:27,570 --> 00:56:27,870
Which is?

1065
00:56:28,030 --> 00:56:31,390
The motivation loop that gets created through tracking your progress.

1066
00:56:31,490 --> 00:56:33,260
Oh, tracking, keeping score.

1067
00:56:33,300 --> 00:56:34,140
Keeping score, yeah.

1068
00:56:34,480 --> 00:56:41,140
Tracking is critical because it neatly bypasses the need for that unreliable spark of motivation we talked about earlier.

1069
00:56:41,400 --> 00:56:41,990
How does it do that?

1070
00:56:42,650 --> 00:56:45,950
It provides immediate, small successes.

1071
00:56:45,930 --> 00:56:54,920
When you physically check off a task on your list, okay, I practiced pronunciation for 10 minutes today, your brain gets a small satisfying hit of completion dopamine.

1072
00:56:55,420 --> 00:56:56,260
Ah, okay.

1073
00:56:56,280 --> 00:56:57,450
So you get a reward right away.

1074
00:56:57,550 --> 00:56:58,470
You get a reward right away.

1075
00:56:58,850 --> 00:57:00,430
That small success feels good.

1076
00:57:00,630 --> 00:57:01,730
Yes, I did it today.

1077
00:57:01,770 --> 00:57:07,740
And that feeling gives you the internal motivation, the reinforcement to do it again tomorrow.

1078
00:57:07,940 --> 00:57:09,740
Which reinforces the discipline behavior.

1079
00:57:09,840 --> 00:57:10,340
Exactly.

1080
00:57:10,480 --> 00:57:12,680
Until it eventually forms an unbreakable habit.

1081
00:57:12,780 --> 00:57:14,790
It creates its own positive feedback loop.

1082
00:57:14,820 --> 00:57:15,420
Absolutely.

1083
00:57:15,460 --> 00:57:23,860
That feeling of accomplishment, even for a tiny step, reinforces the behavior far more effectively than waiting for some grand mood to strike you.

1084
00:57:24,450 --> 00:57:25,470
Much more reliable.

1085
00:57:25,530 --> 00:57:31,460
And one important reminder, often highlighted in the sources, is about the power of setting small starting goals.

1086
00:57:31,950 --> 00:57:33,490
Don't aim too high initially.

1087
00:57:33,530 --> 00:57:38,550
Yes, never underestimate the power of the small goal, especially when building discipline.

1088
00:57:39,260 --> 00:57:44,860
Start with something like, I will learn 10 new English vocabulary words related to finance this week.

1089
00:57:44,880 --> 00:57:45,740
That feels doable.

1090
00:57:45,910 --> 00:57:50,190
Or even, I will spend just five minutes reading a financial abstract in English today.

1091
00:57:51,150 --> 00:57:58,280
These small, simple, clearly manageable wins build your competence, yes, but also your confidence incrementally.

1092
00:57:58,460 --> 00:58:00,120
And they create a kind of success spiral.

1093
00:58:00,260 --> 00:58:00,870
Precisely.

1094
00:58:01,070 --> 00:58:06,590
Success builds confidence, and that confidence then fuels further discipline to take on slightly bigger steps.

1095
00:58:07,000 --> 00:58:08,020
It snowballs upwards.

1096
00:58:08,420 --> 00:58:09,020
Okay.

1097
00:58:09,040 --> 00:58:11,500
So for you listening, set smart goals.

1098
00:58:12,120 --> 00:58:18,690
Create a simple written plan with tiny steps and take those small, steady, trackable steps every single day.

1099
00:58:18,760 --> 00:58:23,220
And over time, the synergy between your clear goals and your automated habits strengthens.

1100
00:58:23,840 --> 00:58:28,070
And your success, whether in English or business, it gets closer much faster than you might anticipate.

1101
00:58:28,430 --> 00:58:28,590
Right.

1102
00:58:28,610 --> 00:58:30,910
Now, we absolutely have to acknowledge the reality here.

1103
00:58:31,310 --> 00:58:34,600
Building self-discipline is, well, it's often a struggle, isn't it?

1104
00:58:34,880 --> 00:58:35,400
It really is.

1105
00:58:35,460 --> 00:58:37,260
We all face that internal resistance.

1106
00:58:37,300 --> 00:58:41,000
Sometimes it's called the resistance with a capital R.

1107
00:58:41,050 --> 00:58:41,410
Yeah.

1108
00:58:42,110 --> 00:58:49,880
And the sources identify broadly four common walls, psychological, sometimes logistical, that stop people dead in their tracks.

1109
00:58:50,300 --> 00:58:50,560
Right.

1110
00:58:50,680 --> 00:58:55,570
And crucially, we need specific sourced strategies to actually overcome them.

1111
00:58:56,130 --> 00:58:58,210
It's not enough just to know they exist.

1112
00:58:58,370 --> 00:58:59,990
OK, let's tackle them one by one.

1113
00:59:00,370 --> 00:59:01,160
Challenge number one.

1114
00:59:02,220 --> 00:59:05,600
Good old fashioned laziness or maybe just inertia.

1115
00:59:05,880 --> 00:59:10,790
That feeling where the task just seems too big or too hard or frankly, just plain boring.

1116
00:59:11,110 --> 00:59:13,010
And it leads straight to procrastination.

1117
00:59:13,530 --> 00:59:14,230
Why does that happen?

1118
00:59:14,550 --> 00:59:22,440
It often happens because the brain naturally resists initiating anything that it perceives as requiring a high mental cost, a lot of effort.

1119
00:59:22,540 --> 00:59:23,920
Okay, so trying to conserve energy.

1120
00:59:23,980 --> 00:59:24,600
Basically, yes.

1121
00:59:24,700 --> 00:59:28,880
And this is where we bring in the sort of technical strategy behind the five-minute trick or the five-minute rule.

1122
00:59:28,990 --> 00:59:29,200
Right.

1123
00:59:29,240 --> 00:59:33,990
This sounds almost childishly simple, but you're saying it's actually scientifically grounded.

1124
00:59:34,270 --> 00:59:34,680
It is.

1125
00:59:34,820 --> 00:59:37,820
It's entirely grounded in the concept of activation energy.

1126
00:59:38,020 --> 00:59:40,020
Activation energy, like in chemistry.

1127
00:59:40,040 --> 00:59:41,220
Exactly like in chemistry.

1128
00:59:41,470 --> 00:59:48,840
Think of activation energy as that high initial friction or the energy barrier that's required to start a complex chemical reaction.

1129
00:59:48,860 --> 00:59:52,000
OK. Or in our case, a difficult or unpleasant task.

1130
00:59:52,580 --> 00:59:58,230
Your brain looks at the large goal, write that entire report or study for an hour.

1131
00:59:58,270 --> 01:00:01,380
It calculates the high mental cost of just starting.

1132
01:00:01,440 --> 01:00:27,150
it automatically wants to avoid it nope too hard precisely it recoils yeah the five minute trick works by telling your brain something different it says okay brain relax i only have to do this for five minutes just five and five minutes feels like such a low barrier such a small cost that the brain doesn't bother putting up a big fight correct the perceived activation energy is drastically lowered the goal isn't to finish the task in five minutes

1133
01:00:27,130 --> 01:00:32,450
The goal is simply to start and successfully get over that initial activation energy barrier.

1134
01:00:32,630 --> 01:00:34,200
And what usually happens after five minutes?

1135
01:00:34,520 --> 01:00:45,710
Well, often once you're actually five minutes into the task, whether it's practicing a tricky English grammar point or maybe starting to write the introduction to that difficult email, you've built up enough momentum.

1136
01:00:45,850 --> 01:00:47,450
Or maybe got into a state of flow.

1137
01:00:47,790 --> 01:00:48,230
Or flow.

1138
01:00:48,270 --> 01:00:48,590
Exactly.

1139
01:00:48,630 --> 01:00:49,310
You're engaged.

1140
01:00:49,570 --> 01:00:55,480
And at that point, stopping actually becomes psychologically harder than just continuing for a bit longer.

1141
01:00:56,220 --> 01:01:01,040
The hardest part is almost always just moving from zero to one, starting.

1142
01:01:01,500 --> 01:01:03,170
Okay, five-minute trick for activation energy.

1143
01:01:03,350 --> 01:01:03,630
Got it.

1144
01:01:03,890 --> 01:01:04,050
Right.

1145
01:01:04,070 --> 01:01:05,410
What's the second major challenge?

1146
01:01:05,610 --> 01:01:11,130
The second one is often external, but it requires internal discipline to manage distractions.

1147
01:01:11,570 --> 01:01:11,990
Ah, yes.

1148
01:01:12,460 --> 01:01:17,300
Phones, social media notifications, noisy colleagues, emails popping up constantly.

1149
01:01:17,440 --> 01:01:19,480
They constantly pull our attention away, don't they?

1150
01:01:19,720 --> 01:01:24,270
And they seriously reduce both the quality and the quantity of our focused professional time.

1151
01:01:24,470 --> 01:01:31,840
And in today's global business environment, that ability to maintain focus, it's probably your most valuable professional resource.

1152
01:01:32,160 --> 01:01:32,580
Absolutely.

1153
01:01:32,780 --> 01:01:37,490
So the fix, according to the sources, is to be highly intentional about your environment design.

1154
01:01:37,770 --> 01:01:38,510
Environment design.

1155
01:01:38,530 --> 01:01:39,030
What does that mean?

1156
01:01:39,270 --> 01:01:45,360
It means actively structuring your physical and digital surroundings to make the easy choice, the disciplined choice.

1157
01:01:45,940 --> 01:01:48,050
You make focus the path of least resistance.

1158
01:01:48,490 --> 01:01:51,030
Okay, but practically speaking...

1159
01:01:51,130 --> 01:01:55,740
Say you're in a busy corporate office or even working remotely where demands are constant.

1160
01:01:56,320 --> 01:01:59,310
You can't always just turn your phone off completely, can you?

1161
01:02:00,050 --> 01:02:05,380
You might need to be accessible for urgent client needs or team pings.

1162
01:02:06,110 --> 01:02:09,810
How do you balance discipline with that required connectivity?

1163
01:02:09,880 --> 01:02:13,460
That definitely requires a more nuanced strategy than just turn everything off.

1164
01:02:13,500 --> 01:02:16,190
You need to define specific blocks of deep work time.

1165
01:02:16,630 --> 01:02:18,810
Maybe it's 9 a.m. to 11 a.m. every morning.

1166
01:02:19,290 --> 01:02:20,970
And you communicate clearly to your team.

1167
01:02:21,510 --> 01:02:26,200
Unless it's an absolute emergency, I'm focusing during this time and will respond afterwards.

1168
01:02:26,340 --> 01:02:27,680
So setting expectations is key.

1169
01:02:27,780 --> 01:02:28,180
Crucial.

1170
01:02:28,260 --> 01:02:33,010
And use tools, apps, or browser extensions that block non-essential websites during those blocks.

1171
01:02:33,170 --> 01:02:36,940
And then, yes, physically remove the most tempting distraction, usually the phone.

1172
01:02:36,960 --> 01:02:38,740
But what if you need it for emergencies?

1173
01:02:38,870 --> 01:02:44,160
If it absolutely must be accessible for emergencies, put it face down, maybe across the room.

1174
01:02:44,220 --> 01:02:46,440
So actually getting to it requires physical effort.

1175
01:02:46,540 --> 01:02:49,600
If force is a conscious decision, is this worth getting up for?

1176
01:02:49,660 --> 01:02:52,700
Instead of just an impulsive glance every two minutes.

1177
01:02:53,010 --> 01:02:55,370
Making friction work for you, not against you.

1178
01:02:55,530 --> 01:02:56,070
Exactly.

1179
01:02:56,210 --> 01:02:57,890
Increase the friction for distractions.

1180
01:02:58,810 --> 01:02:58,910
Yeah.

1181
01:02:58,930 --> 01:02:59,570
Okay.

1182
01:02:59,590 --> 01:03:00,450
Challenge number three.

1183
01:03:01,130 --> 01:03:03,040
This is the perennial excuse, isn't it?

1184
01:03:03,680 --> 01:03:04,660
Lack of time.

1185
01:03:05,240 --> 01:03:06,010
I'm just too busy.

1186
01:03:06,230 --> 01:03:10,430
I have absolutely no time for dedicated English study or deep project planning.

1187
01:03:10,480 --> 01:03:14,920
We hear this constantly, but the sources suggest this needs to be fundamentally reframed.

1188
01:03:15,280 --> 01:03:20,830
It's usually not a time problem, it's a prioritization problem, meaning everyone has the same 24 hours in a day.

1189
01:03:21,610 --> 01:03:35,320
The key is managing that time effectively and critically, making time for what is truly important for your long-term goals, which often means having the discipline to say no to non-essential requests or low-value tasks that fill up your schedule.

1190
01:03:35,430 --> 01:03:38,560
So it's about protecting your time for high-priority activities.

1191
01:03:38,690 --> 01:03:38,970
Yes.

1192
01:03:39,450 --> 01:03:46,420
And it's often not about finding a perfect mythical two hour undisturbed slot for study that rarely exists.

1193
01:03:46,860 --> 01:03:52,210
It's more about utilizing those small pockets of time micro learning slots throughout the day.

1194
01:03:52,330 --> 01:03:52,510
Right.

1195
01:03:52,530 --> 01:03:55,340
Like you said, 10 to 15 minutes applied consistently.

1196
01:03:55,640 --> 01:03:57,640
That really does make a huge difference over time, doesn't it?

1197
01:03:57,720 --> 01:04:02,370
Especially for things like learning English vocabulary or reviewing notes just before a meeting.

1198
01:04:02,350 --> 01:04:02,950
Precisely.

1199
01:04:03,370 --> 01:04:15,190
That 15 minutes of disciplined podcast listening during your commute or using a short coffee break to actively review your pronunciation of five key industry terms, it adds up to hours and hours over a month.

1200
01:04:15,210 --> 01:04:22,380
So it's about leveraging the small, often wasted gaps in your day and consciously dedicating them to your long-term goals instead of just letting them disappear.

1201
01:04:22,600 --> 01:04:24,500
Finding and using the in-between moments.

1202
01:04:24,840 --> 01:04:25,140
Okay.

1203
01:04:25,500 --> 01:04:28,870
And finally, challenge number four, giving up too early.

1204
01:04:28,930 --> 01:04:30,070
This happens all the time, right?

1205
01:04:30,370 --> 01:04:33,760
People get discouraged because they don't see fast, spectacular results.

1206
